                    clause9_title:          `Performance Evaluation`,
                    clause10_title:         `Improvement`,
                    clause4_desc: 'Clause 4 requires the organization to understand its context, the needs and expectations of interested parties, and to define the scope of the QMS.<br><br>You must evaluate whether Brindlecote Components has identified internal and external issues affecting its strategic direction and quality objectives, and whether the QMS scope is complete and current.',
                    clause4_evidence: '<strong>Stakeholder Analysis:</strong> Completed in early 2024, covering customers (OEM automotive clients), suppliers, regulators, and employees. Has not been reviewed or updated since creation — 2 years old.<br><br><strong>QMS Scope Document:</strong> Covers stamping, machining, and assembly operations for traditional powertrain components. Does NOT include the EV components division launched 6 months ago, which now represents 18% of revenue.<br><br><strong>External Context:</strong> SWOT analysis references pre-pandemic market conditions. No mention of semiconductor supply chain disruptions, EV transition trends, or updated regulatory landscape (IATF 16949 changes).<br><br><strong>Process Map:</strong> Exists but shows organizational structure from 2023. Two new departments (EV Engineering, Digital Quality) not represented.',
                    clause4_fb_good: 'Correct assessment. The outdated stakeholder analysis and incomplete QMS scope are significant non-conformities. An auditor would flag the exclusion of the EV division as a major finding — you cannot certify a QMS that does not cover 18% of revenue.',
                    clause4_fb_mid: 'Your rating is in the right range, but consider the severity more carefully. The QMS scope gap alone (excluding the EV division) would likely be raised as a major non-conformity in a Stage 2 audit.',
                    clause4_fb_off: 'This assessment does not match expert findings. The combination of outdated stakeholder analysis, incomplete scope, and obsolete context analysis represents serious gaps. A certification body would raise major non-conformities here.',
                    clause5_desc: 'Clause 5 addresses top management commitment, quality policy, and organizational roles. Management must demonstrate leadership by ensuring the QMS achieves intended results, promoting process approach and risk-based thinking, and ensuring resources are available.<br><br>Evaluate whether senior leadership at Brindlecote Components actively drives the quality management system or merely endorses it on paper.',
                    clause5_evidence: '<strong>Quality Policy:</strong> Signed by the CEO, framed and displayed in the reception area and on the company intranet. Well-written, referencing customer focus, continual improvement, and compliance.<br><br><strong>Management Review:</strong> Procedure MR-001 defines quarterly management reviews. However, the last completed management review was 8 months ago. Three scheduled reviews have no minutes, agendas, or attendance records.<br><br><strong>Quality Objectives:</strong> Corporate-level objectives exist (reduce defects by 15%, improve OTD to 98%). These have NOT been cascaded to department or team level. No individual performance metrics linked to quality.<br><br><strong>Employee Interviews:</strong> Random interviews with 10 shop floor operators — only 2 could articulate the quality policy. 6 were unaware of quality objectives. Management presence on the shop floor described as "rare" by supervisors.',
                    clause5_fb_good: 'Excellent assessment. The gap between documented policy and actual leadership engagement is the critical finding here. Having a quality policy on the wall means nothing if management reviews are skipped and employees cannot describe it.',
                    clause5_fb_mid: 'You identified issues, but the severity may be understated. Missing 3 consecutive management reviews is a systemic failure of leadership commitment, not a minor oversight.',
                    clause5_fb_off: 'This clause is weaker than your rating suggests. Despite good documentation, the 8-month gap in management reviews and poor employee awareness of quality policy indicate fundamental leadership disengagement from the QMS.',
                    clause6_desc: 'Clause 6 requires the organization to address risks and opportunities, establish quality objectives, and plan changes to the QMS. Risk-based thinking should be integrated into planning processes, not treated as a standalone exercise.<br><br>Assess whether Brindlecote Components has a functioning planning system that identifies risks, sets measurable objectives, and manages change effectively.',
                    clause6_evidence: '<strong>Risk Register:</strong> Created during initial QMS setup in 2022. Contains 24 risks. Has never been reviewed or updated. Does not include risks from the EV product launch, new supplier onboarding, or workforce skill gaps.<br><br><strong>Quality Objectives:</strong> Documented at corporate level with targets. However, no action plans exist showing HOW objectives will be achieved — no assigned responsibilities, no timelines, no resource allocations.<br><br><strong>Change Management:</strong> Procedure CM-003 describes change management for QMS modifications. In practice, three major changes occurred in the past year (new product line, reorganization, new ERP system) with no change management records. The procedure has never been followed.<br><br><strong>Opportunities:</strong> No formal process for identifying or capturing opportunities. The standard requires addressing both risks AND opportunities.',
                    clause6_fb_good: 'Strong assessment. The stale risk register and unfollowed change management procedure are hallmarks of a QMS that was built for certification but never integrated into operations. The absence of opportunity identification is often missed — good catch.',
                    clause6_fb_mid: 'Partially correct, but the planning gaps are deeper than your rating suggests. A risk register that has not been touched since creation provides zero value. Combined with no change management records despite major changes, this is a systemic gap.',
                    clause6_fb_off: 'This area is much weaker than your assessment indicates. Every sub-element of Clause 6 has significant gaps: stale risk register, objectives without action plans, change management procedure that exists only on paper.',
                    clause7_desc: 'Clause 7 covers resources, competence, awareness, communication, and documented information. The organization must determine and provide the resources needed, ensure personnel competence, and control documented information.<br><br>Evaluate the support infrastructure at Brindlecote Components — equipment, training, document control, and internal communication systems.',
                    clause7_evidence: '<strong>Calibration:</strong> All measurement equipment (CMMs, gauges, test rigs) has current calibration certificates. Calibration lab is UKAS-accredited. Records well-maintained. This is a strong area.<br><br><strong>Training Records:</strong> HR maintains a training matrix. However, 30% of production operators lack documented evidence of competence for their current roles. 12 operators transferred to the EV line with no retraining records.<br><br><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> Facilities well-maintained. Preventive maintenance program in place with good records. Recent investment in new CNC equipment.<br><br><strong>Document Control:</strong> Electronic document management system, DMS, in use. However, version control is inconsistent — 4 instances found where obsolete procedures were accessible alongside current versions. No formal process for external documents (customer specs, standards).<br><br><strong>Communication:</strong> Monthly team briefings documented. Quality alerts system functional. No formal process for communicating QMS changes to affected parties.',
                    clause7_fb_good: 'Well-calibrated assessment. This clause has genuine strengths (calibration, infrastructure) mixed with real gaps (competence records, document control). The amber classification correctly reflects partial compliance.',
                    clause7_fb_mid: 'Close, but look at the balance of evidence more carefully. Calibration and infrastructure are strong, but the 30% competence gap and version control issues pull the overall score down. Neither fully green nor fully red.',
                    clause7_fb_off: 'Your assessment does not match the mixed evidence. This clause has clear strengths (calibration is excellent) and clear weaknesses (competence records, document versioning). An auditor would see amber with specific minor non-conformities.',
                    clause8_desc: 'Clause 8 is the largest clause, covering operational planning, requirements for products and services, design and development, control of externally provided processes, production, release, and control of nonconforming outputs.<br><br>Assess the operational processes at Brindlecote Components — from customer requirements through production to delivery and nonconformance handling.',
                    clause8_evidence: '<strong>Production Processes:</strong> Well-defined work instructions for all standard product lines. Statistical process control (SPC) implemented on critical dimensions. Process capability studies (Cpk > 1.33) maintained for key characteristics.<br><br><strong>Customer Requirements:</strong> Contract review procedure in place. Customer-specific requirements tracked in a database. However, 2 recent customer complaints relate to requirements that were not captured during contract review.<br><br><strong>Design and Development:</strong> The company recently began designing EV components in-house. Design process exists but lacks formal design review gates. No documented design validation for the 3 EV parts currently in production. FMEA completed but not updated after design changes.<br><br><strong>Supplier Management:</strong> Approved supplier list maintained. Annual evaluations conducted. Incoming inspection data tracked. However, 2 suppliers on the approved list have not been evaluated in 18 months.<br><br><strong>Nonconforming Product:</strong> NCR process well-established with quarantine procedures. 98% of NCRs closed within target time. Strong area overall.',
                    clause8_fb_good: 'Accurate assessment. Operations is the strongest area, reflecting a company with good manufacturing discipline. The gaps in design validation for EV parts and missed contract review requirements are real but contained issues.',
                    clause8_fb_mid: 'Reasonable, but pay attention to the design and development gaps. Missing design validation for parts already in production is a significant finding that prevents a fully green assessment.',
                    clause8_fb_off: 'Your assessment is misaligned with the evidence. Production processes and NCR management are genuinely strong, but design and development for the new EV line has notable gaps that require attention.',
                    clause9_desc: 'Clause 9 requires the organization to monitor, measure, analyze, and evaluate QMS performance. This includes customer satisfaction, internal audits, and management review — the "Check" in the PDCA cycle.<br><br>Evaluate whether Brindlecote Components has effective performance evaluation mechanisms and whether the data drives action.',
                    clause9_evidence: '<strong>Customer Satisfaction:</strong> Quarterly surveys sent to top 20 customers. Response rate around 60%. Satisfaction score trending from 7.8 to 7.2 over the past year. The declining trend has not been formally analyzed or actioned.<br><br><strong>Internal Audits:</strong> Audit procedure exists. Last complete internal audit cycle was 14 months ago. The 2025 audit program was created but no audits have been conducted. Two trained internal auditors left the company and have not been replaced.<br><br><strong>KPIs:</strong> Production KPIs tracked daily (OEE, scrap rate, OTD). Quality objective KPIs exist at corporate level but are only reviewed when someone asks — no regular reporting cadence. No trend analysis performed.<br><br><strong>Management Review:</strong> As noted in Clause 5, the last management review with complete records was 8 months ago. Required inputs (audit results, customer feedback, process performance, risk status) have not been compiled since.',
                    clause9_fb_good: 'Precisely right. Performance evaluation is one of the weakest areas. The 14-month audit gap alone is a major non-conformity. Combined with declining customer satisfaction that nobody has analyzed and missing management reviews, this clause would generate multiple findings.',
                    clause9_fb_mid: 'Your assessment is too generous. A 14-month gap in internal audits when the standard requires them at planned intervals (typically annual) is a clear major non-conformity. Add the management review gaps, and this is solidly red.',
                    clause9_fb_off: 'This is significantly weaker than your rating indicates. Lapsed internal audits, unanalyzed declining customer satisfaction, and absent management reviews represent a fundamental breakdown in the performance evaluation system.',
                    clause10_desc: 'Clause 10 requires the organization to determine and select opportunities for improvement and implement necessary actions. This includes corrective action, continual improvement, and responding to nonconformities.<br><br>Assess whether Brindlecote Components has a functioning improvement culture or merely a corrective action procedure.',
                    clause10_evidence: '<strong>Corrective Action Process:</strong> Procedure CA-001 is well-written with clear steps: containment, root cause analysis, corrective action, verification, effectiveness check.<br><br><strong>Open CARs:</strong> 12 corrective action requests currently open. Average age: 7 months. Only 3 of 12 have documented root cause analysis. The remaining 9 list the root cause as "operator error" or "lack of training" with no supporting analysis (no 5-Why, no fishbone, no data).<br><br><strong>Effectiveness Reviews:</strong> Of the 15 CARs closed in the past year, only 4 have documented effectiveness verification. The rest were closed administratively after the action was taken, with no check that the problem was actually resolved.<br><br><strong>Continual Improvement:</strong> A suggestion box program exists. 3 suggestions submitted in the past year (all from the same employee). No formal continual improvement methodology (no Kaizen events, no lean projects, no Six Sigma). No evidence of using data from Clause 9 to drive improvement.<br><br><strong>Preventive Thinking:</strong> No evidence of proactive improvement initiatives. All actions are reactive — triggered by customer complaints or nonconformances.',
